<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Valle Crucis Abbey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1201–<a href="/years/1537">1537</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Valle Crucis Abbey is a ruined Cistercian abbey near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llangollen">Llangollen</a> in north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, founded in 1201 by Madog ap Gruffudd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maelor">Maelor</a>, prince of northern Powys. Its name, 'valley of the cross', comes from the nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pillar-of-eliseg">Pillar of Eliseg</a>. It was a centre of Welsh learning and poetry in the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and was dissolved in 1537.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/valle-crucis-abbey.jpg" alt="Valle Crucis Abbey" width="320" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Llywelyn2000 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mynydd_Felfed_(Coed_Hyrddyn)_-_Velvet_Hill,_Llantysilio,_Llangollen,_Sir_Ddinbych,_Cymru_(Wales)_70.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
